\subsection{Time is part of authority, not a decorative field}
The executable acceptance condition for each active warrant $w$ is
\begin{equation}
 w.c=c_{\mathrm{current}},\qquad
 w.t_{\mathrm{start}}\leq t<w.t_{\mathrm{end}},\qquad
 a\in w.\mathcal A.
\end{equation}
Both the evidence and the owner's grant must pass. Explicit not-before and expiry checks are established engineering practice; RFC~7519 separates these temporal conditions for token processing \citep{jwt}. Our local witness uses integer logical ticks, not JWTs, and makes no standards-compliance claim.

Adversarial review of edition 1.0.0 found that its code stored a grant's expiry but omitted its start: a grant issued at time 5 incorrectly permitted a query for time 1. It also retained an older active warrant after a failed requalification attempt. Edition 1.1.0 stores both endpoints, rejects backward state transitions and malformed times/scopes, and clears current proof and authority when requalification begins. The original release remains available as the defect's historical source. The strengthened suite includes 671 interval-boundary assertions; these checks do not establish a trusted clock, authenticated principals, or complete mediation outside the local process.

